# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in your own key.
# `.env.local` is git-ignored, so your key never leaves your machine.
#
# Get a free key at https://unsplash.com/developers (register an app, then
# copy the "Access Key" — NOT the Secret Key).
#
# Vite only exposes variables prefixed with `VITE_` to the browser, and reads
# them at BUILD time — the values are baked into `dist/`. This file and
# `.env.local` are for local dev only; a deployed copy needs the same keys set
# wherever it builds (on Vercel or Netlify, the host's Environment Variables
# settings) and a redeploy afterwards, or the features below stay off there.
# Note: because this app has no backend, this key IS visible in the built
# JS bundle. That's fine for a personal homepage you host yourself, but
# don't publish the build somewhere public with a key you care about.
#
# If you leave this blank, the app quietly falls back to the small set of
# gradient backgrounds bundled in `src/assets/backgrounds/`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VITE_UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: accounts and cloud sync via Supabase. Leave both blank to use the
# app entirely offline with localStorage, same as always — the sign-in
# button in the header simply doesn't appear.
#
# 1. Create a free project at https://supabase.com.
# 2. Run the SQL in README.md's "Adding accounts" section in the project's
#    SQL editor — it creates the table these two values let the app read
#    and write, scoped to each signed-in user.
# 3. Project Settings -> API for the URL and the anon/public key.
#
# The anon key is safe to expose in client code by design — it identifies
# the project, not a user. What actually protects each user's data is the
# Row Level Security policy from that SQL, not secrecy of this key. Same
# "safe to ship, no backend needed" reasoning as the Unsplash key above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: let people store FILES (images, avatars, attachments) in their own
# cloud storage. Leave blank and the provider isn't offered; leave both blank
# and the Settings -> Files tab doesn't appear at all.
#
# This has nothing to do with where bookmarks and settings sync — those go to
# Supabase when signed in and stay on the device otherwise, whatever is set
# here. Connecting storage just gives the app somewhere to put files, on the
# user's own quota rather than yours.
#
# Dropbox: create an app at https://www.dropbox.com/developers/apps
#
#   - Access type: "App folder" (NOT "Full Dropbox" — the point is that this
#     app can only ever touch its own folder). The app's NAME becomes the
#     folder name under Apps/, so renaming it later moves that folder for
#     everyone already connected.
#
#   - Permissions tab: tick account_info.read, files.content.read and
#     files.content.write, then press Submit. They are OFF by default, and
#     missing them gives you the confusing failure: the connection succeeds
#     and then every request 403s. Changing scopes later means everyone
#     already connected has to reconnect.
#
#   - Settings tab -> Redirect URIs: paste one full URL and press "Add", once
#     per origin. These are matched exactly, so they are real URLs, not
#     patterns — literally:
#         http://localhost:5173/oauth-callback.html
#         https://your-app.vercel.app/oauth-callback.html
#     Dropbox requires https for everything except http://localhost, so the
#     dev one above is fine as-is. BOTH are needed if you deploy: registering
#     only localhost gets you a working dev setup and a deployed app that
#     fails at the consent screen with "invalid redirect_uri". Because the
#     match is exact, preview deployments (Vercel gives each push a new URL)
#     won't work unless you add each one or point at a stable alias —
#     production and localhost are the ones that matter.
#
#   - Settings tab -> "Allow public clients (Implicit Grant & PKCE)": Allow.
#     Required, and currently the default. It is what lets the browser finish
#     PKCE with no client secret; on "Disallow" every connection fails.
#
#   - Copy the "App key" below. Do NOT copy the App secret — there is nowhere
#     safe to put it in a JS bundle, and PKCE is what makes it unnecessary.
#
#   - Ignore the rest of that page. "Generated access token" is the trap: it
#     mints a long-lived token for YOUR OWN Dropbox, for curl and scripts, and
#     it is not what goes below — the app gets its own token per user through
#     OAuth. Chooser / Saver / Embedder domains are for Dropbox's drop-in JS
#     widgets, which this app doesn't use. Webhook URIs need a server to
#     receive POSTs, and there isn't one.
#
# Heads up: a Dropbox app stays in development mode until you apply for
# production, which caps it at ~50 linked accounts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VITE_DROPBOX_APP_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Drive: create an OAuth client at https://console.cloud.google.com
#
#   - Enable the Google Drive API for the project
#
#   - Credentials -> Create OAuth client ID -> Web application
#
#   - Authorised JavaScript origins: paste one per origin you use — literally:
#         http://localhost:5173
#         https://your-app.vercel.app
#     ORIGINS, not callback URLs: no path, no trailing slash. Unlike Dropbox
#     above there is NO redirect URI to configure here, and the field for one
#     should be left empty — Google Identity Services hands the token back to
#     the page instead of redirecting, so oauth-callback.html isn't involved.
#     Same exact-match caveat as Dropbox: per-push preview URLs won't work
#     unless you add them.
#
#   - OAuth consent screen -> add the scope
#     https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file
#
#   - Copy the "Client ID" below. The client secret is NOT used and must not
#     go in here: this app uses the browser token flow precisely because a
#     secret can't live in a JS bundle.
#
# `drive.file` only reaches files this app created, and Google classes it as
# non-sensitive — so unlike the broader Drive scopes it doesn't put the app
# behind verification or a test-user cap. The consent screen in the console
# shows the current classification for whatever scopes you add.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
